WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.716
A few moves turn the Patch Bay into an instrument.

2
00:00:03.716 --> 00:00:05.948
First: one parameter driving another.

3
00:00:06.399 --> 00:00:09.966
Every parameter has an output jack as well as an input.

4
00:00:09.966 --> 00:00:13.274
Drag the output of Z Push onto the input of H Size.

5
00:00:13.274 --> 00:00:19.177
Now the width follows the depth, and a Param Out node carries the signal behind the scenes.

6
00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:21.942
Right-click empty space, and add a Note.

7
00:00:21.942 --> 00:00:22.578
Type on it.

8
00:00:22.578 --> 00:00:27.203
Notes are labels that live in the patch, so you can explain what a patch is for.

9
00:00:27.653 --> 00:00:31.580
Restore the window, and open Modulation in the Controls panel.

10
00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:34.051
The cable you just made is a row there.

11
00:00:34.051 --> 00:00:38.106
The Patch Bay and the Controls panel are two views of one patch.

12
00:00:38.557 --> 00:00:43.909
For two displays, the pop-out button on a window sends it to a browser window of its own.

13
00:00:43.909 --> 00:00:46.855
It stays live, and the main window keeps working.

14
00:00:47.311 --> 00:00:51.524
Save a preset or a project, and every cable and note goes with it.

15
00:00:51.524 --> 00:00:55.673
Open it later, here or on another machine, and it all comes back.

16
00:00:56.123 --> 00:00:57.413
That is the Patch Bay.

17
00:00:57.413 --> 00:00:59.643
The next level up is the Graph engine.
